The solution was to use the version of zic we already had and modify our=20
existing timezone data. The HP DST file (contents quoted below: thanks Davi=
d)=20
helped.

Two minor issues:=20

1) our zdump choked on the leading colon when specifying the timezone but=20
worked when it was omitted.=20
2) "zic backward" failed.

Note: We have not tried rolling the clock ahead to test how the system acts=
=20
during the new daylight savings period as yet. Caveat emptor.

Thanks again.

Contents of the HP DST file:

__________

The following steps will implement the 2007 changes to U.S. Daylight Saving=
=20
Time (DST) rules on Tru64 UNIX version 5 systems for which an Early Release=
=20
Patch (ERP) is not available.  An ERP to implement these changes is availab=
le=20
for V5.1A with patch kit 6, for V5.1B with patch kit 4 (aka V5.1B-2), and=20
V5.1B with patch kit 5 (aka V5.1B-3).  For all other V5 version/patch kit=20
combinations, the changes must be implemented manually.  This is a reasonab=
ly=20
quick and straightforward process and does not require a system reboot.


Step 1: For comparison purposes, display the times for DST changes for 2006=
=20
and 2007 under the current rules.  This can be done using any of the United=
=20
States time zones; for this example, we will use America/New_York.  To veri=
fy=20
the time zone in use on your system, display the symbolic link=20
/etc/zoneinfo/localtime:

# ls -l /etc/zoneinfo/localtime
lrwxrwxrwx   1 root     system        18 Feb  8  2004 /etc/zoneinfo/localti=
me=20
=2D> ./America/New_York

Now use zdump to show the DST transitions for this time zone in 2006-2007:

# zdump -v :America/New_York | grep =E2=80=98200[67]=E2=80=99
:America/New_York  Fri Oct 20 14:59:45 2006 EDT
:America/New_York  Sun Apr  2 06:59:59 2006 GMT =3D Sun Apr  2 01:59:59 200=
6 EST=20
isdst=3D0 gmtoff=3D-18000
:America/New_York  Sun Apr  2 07:00:00 2006 GMT =3D Sun Apr  2 03:00:00 200=
6 EDT=20
isdst=3D1 gmtoff=3D-14400
:America/New_York  Sun Oct 29 05:59:59 2006 GMT =3D Sun Oct 29 01:59:59 200=
6 EDT=20
isdst=3D1 gmtoff=3D-14400
:America/New_York  Sun Oct 29 06:00:00 2006 GMT =3D Sun Oct 29 01:00:00 200=
6 EST=20
isdst=3D0 gmtoff=3D-18000
:America/New_York  Sun Apr  1 06:59:59 2007 GMT =3D Sun Apr  1 01:59:59 200=
7 EST=20
isdst=3D0 gmtoff=3D-18000
:America/New_York  Sun Apr  1 07:00:00 2007 GMT =3D Sun Apr  1 03:00:00 200=
7 EDT=20
isdst=3D1 gmtoff=3D-14400
:America/New_York  Sun Oct 28 05:59:59 2007 GMT =3D Sun Oct 28 01:59:59 200=
7 EDT=20
isdst=3D1 gmtoff=3D-14400
:America/New_York  Sun Oct 28 06:00:00 2007 GMT =3D Sun Oct 28 01:00:00 200=
7 EST=20
isdst=3D0 gmtoff=3D-18000

The first line of output shows the current system time.  Each subsequent pa=
ir=20
of lines shows the time just before and just after a DST transition.  The=20
first pair shows the spring transition to DST on the first Sunday in April,=
=20
while the second pair shows the fall transition to Standard time on the las=
t=20
Sunday in October.  This shows that the current DST rules are in effect for=
=20
both 2006 and 2007.  Save this output for later comparison.


Step 2: Modify the time zone source file that contains the US time zone rul=
es. =20
=46irst, save a backup copy of this file:

# cd /etc/zoneinfo/sources
# cp northamerica northamerica.bak

Now edit file =E2=80=9Cnorthamerica=E2=80=9D with an editor of your choice =
and find the=20
section of lines that begin with =E2=80=9CRule      US=E2=80=9D.  This sect=
ion should look=20
like this:

# Rule  NAME    FROM    TO      TYPE    IN      ON      AT      SAVE   =20
